============================================================
1. FIND ALL EMPLOYEES
============================================================

Input

Employee

EmpId | Name  | DeptId | Salary
------+-------+--------+-------
1     | John  | 10     | 50000
2     | Mary  | 10     | 70000
3     | Steve | 20     | 60000
4     | Tom   | 20     | 80000
5     | David | 30     | 90000

Query

SELECT *
FROM Employee;

Output

EmpId | Name  | DeptId | Salary
------+-------+--------+-------
1     | John  | 10     | 50000
2     | Mary  | 10     | 70000
3     | Steve | 20     | 60000
4     | Tom   | 20     | 80000
5     | David | 30     | 90000

Explanation

SELECT * returns all columns from the Employee table.
No WHERE condition is used, so all rows are returned.

============================================================
2. FIND EMPLOYEES WITH SALARY GREATER THAN 60000
============================================================

Input

Name  | Salary
------+-------
John  | 50000
Mary  | 70000
Steve | 60000
Tom   | 80000
David | 90000

Query

SELECT Name, Salary
FROM Employee
WHERE Salary > 60000;

Output

Name  | Salary
------+-------
Mary  | 70000
Tom   | 80000
David | 90000

Explanation

WHERE filters individual rows.
Only employees whose salary is greater than 60000 are returned.
Steve is excluded because his salary is exactly 60000.

