============================================================
8. AVERAGE SALARY PER DEPARTMENT
============================================================

Input

DeptId | Employee | Salary
-------+----------+-------
10     | John     | 50000
10     | Mary     | 70000
20     | Steve    | 60000
20     | Tom      | 80000
30     | David    | 90000

Query

SELECT DeptId,
       AVG(Salary) AS AvgSalary
FROM Employee
GROUP BY DeptId;

Output

DeptId | AvgSalary
-------+----------
10     | 60000
20     | 70000
30     | 90000

Explanation

Department 10: (50000 + 70000) / 2 = 60000
Department 20: (60000 + 80000) / 2 = 70000
Department 30: 90000 / 1 = 90000

GROUP BY divides employees by department.
AVG(Salary) calculates the average salary in each group.

============================================================
9. FIND EMPLOYEES ABOVE THE COMPANY AVERAGE SALARY
============================================================

Input

Name  | Salary
------+-------
John  | 50000
Mary  | 70000
Steve | 60000
Tom   | 80000
David | 90000

Company average:

(50000 + 70000 + 60000 + 80000 + 90000) / 5
= 350000 / 5
= 70000

Query

SELECT Name, Salary
FROM Employee
WHERE Salary >
(
    SELECT AVG(Salary)
    FROM Employee
);

Output

Name  | Salary
------+-------
Tom   | 80000
David | 90000

Explanation

The subquery calculates the company average salary as 70000.
The outer query returns employees earning more than 70000.
Mary is excluded because her salary is equal to, not greater than, 70000.

