14. RANK EXAMPLE
============================================================

Input

Name  | Salary
------+-------
David | 90000
Tom   | 80000
Alex  | 80000
Mary  | 70000
Steve | 60000
John  | 50000

Query

SELECT Name,
       Salary,
       RANK() OVER (ORDER BY Salary DESC) AS rnk
FROM Employee;

Output

Name  | Salary | rnk
------+--------+----
David | 90000  | 1
Tom   | 80000  | 2
Alex  | 80000  | 2
Mary  | 70000  | 4
Steve | 60000  | 5
John  | 50000  | 6

Explanation

RANK assigns the same rank to equal salary values.
Tom and Alex both receive rank 2.
The next rank is 4, so rank 3 is skipped.

============================================================
15. CALCULATE A RUNNING TOTAL
============================================================

Input

Name  | Salary
------+-------
John  | 50000
Steve | 60000
Mary  | 70000
Tom   | 80000
David | 90000

Query

SELECT Name,
       Salary,
       SUM(Salary) OVER
       (
           ORDER BY Salary
           ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW
       ) AS RunningTotal
FROM Employee;

Output

Name  | Salary | RunningTotal
------+--------+-------------
John  | 50000  | 50000
Steve | 60000  | 110000
Mary  | 70000  | 180000
Tom   | 80000  | 260000
David | 90000  | 350000

Explanation

The rows are ordered by salary from lowest to highest.
SUM adds the current salary to all preceding salary values.
The explicit ROWS frame ensures a row-by-row running total.

============================================================
16. DEPARTMENT AVERAGE USING PARTITION BY
============================================================

Input

Name  | DeptId | Salary
------+--------+-------
John  | 10     | 50000
Mary  | 10     | 70000
Steve | 20     | 60000
Tom   | 20     | 80000
David | 30     | 90000

Query

SELECT Name,
       Salary,
       AVG(Salary) OVER (PARTITION BY DeptId) AS DeptAvg
FROM Employee;

Output

Name  | Salary | DeptAvg
------+--------+--------
John  | 50000  | 60000
Mary  | 70000  | 60000
Steve | 60000  | 70000
Tom   | 80000  | 70000
David | 90000  | 90000

Explanation

PARTITION BY creates a separate calculation window for each department.
AVG calculates the department average while preserving every employee row.
Unlike GROUP BY, it does not collapse the result into one row per department.

