20. FIND THE TOP THREE HIGHEST SALARIES
============================================================

Input

Name  | Salary
------+-------
John  | 50000
Mary  | 70000
Steve | 60000
Tom   | 80000
David | 90000

Query

SELECT Salary
FROM Employee
ORDER BY Salary DESC
FETCH FIRST 3 ROWS ONLY;

Output

Salary
------
90000
80000
70000

Explanation

ORDER BY Salary DESC sorts salaries from highest to lowest.
FETCH FIRST 3 ROWS ONLY returns the first three rows after sorting.
Depending on the database, equivalent syntax includes LIMIT 3 or TOP 3.

============================================================
30-SECOND SQL REVISION
============================================================

1.  SELECT *           -> Return all columns and rows
2.  WHERE              -> Filter individual rows
3.  DISTINCT           -> Remove duplicate values
4.  GROUP BY           -> Create groups
5.  HAVING             -> Filter groups after aggregation
6.  INNER JOIN         -> Return matching rows
7.  LEFT JOIN          -> Return all left-table rows
8.  AVG()              -> Calculate average value
9.  Subquery           -> Run one query inside another query
10. MAX()              -> Return the highest value
11. Second highest     -> MAX() with a subquery
12. Third highest      -> ROW_NUMBER() with a CTE
13. ROW_NUMBER()       -> Assign unique sequential numbers
14. RANK()             -> Assign ranks and leave gaps after ties
15. Running total      -> SUM() OVER()
16. PARTITION BY       -> Calculate group metrics without collapsing rows
17. Duplicate records  -> GROUP BY with HAVING COUNT(*) > 1
18. UNION              -> Combine results and remove duplicates
19. UNION ALL          -> Combine results and retain duplicates
20. Top N records      -> ORDER BY with FETCH, LIMIT, or TOP

